---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import TreeLab from "../../components/TreeLab.astro";
import Excerpt from "../../components/Excerpt.astro";
import SourceNote from "../../components/SourceNote.astro";
import SourcePanel from "../../components/SourcePanel.astro";
import RoomNav from "../../components/RoomNav.astro";
import { gh } from "../../lib/openzfs";
import { room, roomUrl, roomNumber } from "../../lib/rooms";

// The question-first format (DECISIONS.md D21.1): the question, a plain answer,
// the figure, then the code in Source notes. Links to the code live in the notes only.
const lang = "en";
const r = room("tree");
const VL = "module/zfs/vdev_label.c";
const L = {
  rootSet: gh("module/zfs/dsl_pool.c", 593),
  setdirty: gh("module/zfs/dbuf.c", 2583),
  newAddr: gh("module/zfs/zio.c", 2102, 2104),
  pio: gh("module/zfs/dbuf.c", 5411),
  ready: gh("module/zfs/dbuf.c", 5083, 5085),
  even: gh(VL, 2109),
  ub: gh(VL, 2133),
  odd: gh(VL, 2154),
  dirtyList: gh(VL, 1962, 1984),
  ubWrite: gh(VL, 1792, 1796),
  slotSize: gh("include/sys/vdev_impl.h", 491, 496),
  readConfig: gh(VL, 852, 856),
  labelCksum: gh(VL, 197, 200),
  compare: gh(VL, 1495, 1505),
  load: gh(VL, 1570, 1588),
};
---

<Base
  lang={lang}
  roomId="tree"
  title="What if the power fails while ZFS is writing? The tree and the uberblock · Uberblok"
  description="ZFS writes a new tree of blocks beside the old one, then switches to it with one uberblock write, so a power cut leaves the old pool or the new one, never a mix. Interactive, with the OpenZFS code."
>
  <article class="wrap">
    <header class="room-head">
      <p class="kicker">Room {roomNumber(r.id)} · {r.title.en}</p>
      <h1>What if the power fails while ZFS is writing?</h1>
      <div class="prose answer">
        <p class="lead">
          ZFS gathers changes in memory and writes them out together every few seconds, as a transaction group (txg).
          One txg can write thousands of blocks to several disks, and the power can fail at any moment while it does. A
          filesystem that updates blocks in place can be left half old and half new, and a repair tool such as fsck has
          to walk the disk to fix it. ZFS has no fsck, so after a crash the pool must come back as it was before the txg
          or as it is after it, never as a mix of the two.
        </p>
        <p>
          Every block of a pool hangs from one small record, the uberblock. It holds a single pointer to the top of a
          tree of block pointers, and the tree leads down to every block of data. When a block changes, ZFS writes the new
          version somewhere else and leaves the old one in place. The block above it holds its address, so that block
          gets a new version too, and so on up to the top. Until the end of the txg, the uberblock still points to the old tree, which nothing
          has touched.
        </p>
        <p>
          Once the new tree is written, ZFS makes the disks it wrote to empty their write caches, the memory where a disk
          holds writes before they reach stable storage. Then it writes a new uberblock that points to the new tree, and
          writing that uberblock switches the pool over. The new uberblock goes to a different slot from the one in use,
          so a write cut halfway cannot damage the current one. Each disk keeps four copies of this ring of slots. On
          import, ZFS reads every slot, skips the ones that fail their checksum, and takes the newest. It points to a
          complete tree, old or new.
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="climb">
      <h2 id="climb"><span class="n">1</span> Change a block, follow the pointers up</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The figure draws one file under its uberblock: four data blocks, D0 to D3, two levels of indirect blocks above
          them, the block that holds the file’s dnode (the record that describes the file), and the objset at the top.
          The number under each name is the txg that wrote that version. D2 has just been changed. Its new copy and new
          copies of every block above it stand beside the old ones, still in memory, and the uberblock still points to
          the old tree.
        </p>
        <p>
          Press Next step. ZFS writes the new blocks from the bottom up, one row of the figure per step. A block stores the new
          addresses and checksums of the blocks below it, and they are known only once those blocks are placed. The
          dotted pointers fill in one row at a time, up to the objset. Write D3 before the first step to add it to the same txg. Only
          D3 itself is new, because the blocks above it were already copied for D2.
        </p>
      </div>
      <TreeLab lang={lang} />
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="commit">
      <h2 id="commit"><span class="n">2</span> The commit, in four beats</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Each disk carries four labels, two at its start and two at its end. A label holds the pool’s configuration and
          a ring of uberblock slots. After the objset, keep pressing Next step: the next four steps are the commit, and
          the figure numbers them as beats 1 to 4. First every disk written in the txg empties its write cache, so the
          new tree is on stable storage before anything points to it. Then the even labels, L0 and L2, get the new
          configuration. Then the uberblock goes into the next slot of all four rings, and the arrow at the top of the
          tree moves to the new objset. Last, the odd labels, L1 and L3, get the new configuration.
        </p>
        <p>
          The labels are rewritten only in a txg that changes the configuration, such as one that adds a disk. Turn off
          “The vdev configuration changes in this txg” before the first step to see an ordinary txg, where only the
          uberblock is new. After an import the switch stays on for the next txg, because ZFS rewrites every label once
          it has opened the pool.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="plug">
      <h2 id="plug"><span class="n">3</span> Pull the plug, then import</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Pull the plug at any step, then import the pool. The cut falls during the step that would have come next.
          Before beat 3, the old uberblock is still the newest valid one, so the pool comes back on the old tree, and
          nothing points to the blocks written for the lost txg. If the configuration changed in this txg, the even
          labels written at beat 2 are newer than that uberblock, so the import passes over them and reads an odd label.
          Once one copy of the new uberblock has landed, it wins, and the pool comes back on the new tree. A cut in the
          middle of an uberblock write leaves a torn slot, which fails its checksum and is skipped. To see both cases,
          set “Uberblock writes that land before a cut in beat 3” and pull the plug once the even labels are written. At
          0 the pool comes back on the old tree, and at 1 or more on the new one.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="code">
      <h2 id="code">In the code</h2>
      <p class="prose">
        The figure follows seven places in the OpenZFS source. Open a Source note to see the lines, taken from release
        zfs-2.4.4.
      </p>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>The root</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          The uberblock is a small structure. Among other fields, it holds the txg it commits, a timestamp, and one block
          pointer, <code>ub_rootbp</code>, to the top of the tree.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>struct uberblock</code>, uberblock_impl.h</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path="include/sys/uberblock_impl.h"
            from={122}
            to={128}
            symbol="struct uberblock"
            notes={[
              { from: 125, text: "<code>ub_txg</code>: the txg this uberblock commits. On import, the valid uberblock with the highest txg wins." },
              { from: 127, text: "<code>ub_timestamp</code>: decides between two uberblocks of the same txg." },
              { from: 128, text: `<code>ub_rootbp</code>: the only pointer, to the pool’s meta objset (MOS). <code>dsl_pool_sync_mos</code> <a href="${L.rootSet}">sets it</a> once the MOS is written.` },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>A change marks every parent</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          <code>dbuf_dirty</code> marks a block as changed in the open txg. If the block has a parent, it finds the parent
          one level up and calls itself on it. That is why one changed block gives a new copy of every block on its path
          to the top.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>dbuf_dirty</code>, dbuf.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path="module/zfs/dbuf.c"
            from={2537}
            to={2552}
            symbol="dbuf_dirty"
            notes={[
              { from: 2537, text: "A block below the top level of its object has a parent indirect block." },
              { from: 2542, to: 2545, text: "The parent is at level + 1, block <code>db_blkid &gt;&gt; epbs</code>, with 2<sup>epbs</sup> pointers per indirect block (1024 for 128 KiB). The figure uses 2, to stay small." },
              { from: 2552, text: `The parent is marked too, and so on up. The top level is held by the dnode, which is <a href="${L.setdirty}">marked dirty</a> in its own block of dnodes. At sync, every marked block is written to <a href="${L.newAddr}">a new place</a>.` },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>Children before parents</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          The write of a block is an I/O whose children are the writes of the blocks it points to.
          <code>zio_write_compress</code> holds the parent back until every child is ready, which means the child has its
          new address and checksum. The parent is then checksummed with those new pointers in it.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>zio_write_compress</code>, zio.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path="module/zfs/zio.c"
            from={1932}
            to={1939}
            symbol="zio_write_compress"
            notes={[
              { from: 1932, to: 1939, text: `The parent’s write stops at this stage until its children are ready, then runs this stage again. <code>dbuf_write</code> makes each block’s write <a href="${L.pio}">a child of its parent’s</a>, and when a child is ready, <code>dbuf_write_ready</code> <a href="${L.ready}">copies its new block pointer into the parent</a>. The tree is written from the data up.` },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>Flush before the uberblock</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          <code>vdev_config_sync</code> runs the commit. It starts by flushing the write cache of every disk written in the
          txg, and its comment says why.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>vdev_config_sync</code>, vdev_label.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={VL}
            from={2085}
            to={2098}
            symbol="vdev_config_sync"
            notes={[
              { from: 2085, to: 2090, text: "The blocks of this txg must be on stable storage “before any uberblock that references them”." },
              { from: 2093, to: 2098, text: `Every disk written in this txg is flushed, and the wait covers them all. Then come <a href="${L.even}">the even labels</a>, <a href="${L.ub}">the uberblocks</a> and <a href="${L.odd}">the odd labels</a>. The labels are written only for <a href="${L.dirtyList}">vdevs whose configuration changed</a>.` },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>A new slot every txg</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          <code>vdev_uberblock_sync</code> picks the slot from the txg number, so the new uberblock goes to a different slot
          from the one the pool is using.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>vdev_uberblock_sync</code>, vdev_label.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={VL}
            from={1782}
            to={1784}
            symbol="vdev_uberblock_sync"
            notes={[
              { from: 1782, to: 1784, text: `The slot is <code>ub_txg</code> modulo the number of slots: 128 on disks with 512-byte sectors, <a href="${L.slotSize}">32 on 4K sectors</a>. Multihost keeps one slot back. An uberblock that updates the current txg during a RAID-Z expansion takes the slot before, still not the one in use. The same slot is <a href="${L.ubWrite}">written in all four labels</a>.` },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>The two cases of a cut</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          The comment on the uberblock step names the two cases that the figure’s import replays.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>vdev_config_sync</code>, the uberblock step</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={VL}
            from={2118}
            to={2131}
            symbol="vdev_config_sync"
            notes={[
              { from: 2123, to: 2126, text: `No new uberblock landed: the previous one wins. The even labels are newer than it, and the import <a href="${L.readConfig}">reads the newest label not newer than the uberblock</a>, an odd one.` },
              { from: 2128, to: 2131, text: "One or more landed: the new uberblock wins, and the even labels written just before match it." },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h3>The import keeps the newest valid uberblock</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          On import, <code>vdev_uberblock_load_done</code> runs once for every slot of every label. A slot that fails its
          checksum or has the wrong magic number is skipped. Among the others, the highest txg wins, then the latest
          timestamp.
        </p>
        <SourceNote lang={lang}>
          <Fragment slot="label"><code>vdev_uberblock_load_done</code>, vdev_label.c</Fragment>
          <Excerpt
            lang={lang}
            path={VL}
            from={1547}
            to={1553}
            symbol="vdev_uberblock_load_done"
            notes={[
              { from: 1547, text: `A slot counts only if its read succeeded, and the read <a href="${L.labelCksum}">checks the slot’s own checksum</a>, so a torn write is skipped. <code>uberblock_verify</code> checks the magic number. The function runs for <a href="${L.load}">every slot of every label</a>.` },
              { from: 1552, to: 1553, text: `The best uberblock so far is replaced when <a href="${L.compare}"><code>vdev_uberblock_compare</code></a> ranks the new one higher. <code>spa_load_max_txg</code> is lowered only when an import rewinds to an older txg.` },
            ]}
          />
        </SourceNote>
      </div>
      <SourcePanel lang={lang} />
    </section>

    <section class="section simplified" aria-labelledby="simplified">
      <h2 id="simplified">What the figure leaves out</h2>
      <p class="prose muted">
        The figure shows one file and very few blocks: two pointers per indirect block instead of up to 1024. It folds
        the path from the MOS to a filesystem’s own objset into one block of dnodes. In the code, the writes of one level
        overlap, and a child counts as ready once it has its address and checksum, before its data reaches the disk. The
        figure finishes one row per step, and the flush is what puts every block on stable storage. All of this assumes
        that a disk really writes its cache to stable storage when ZFS asks it to. There is one disk, so every uberblock
        and every label lands on it, and its rings have the 128 slots of 512-byte sectors. In a pool with more than three
        top-level vdevs, an ordinary txg writes the uberblock to three of them, chosen at random. The writes
        of one commit step are issued together in the code. The figure lands them in order and tears the one in flight
        when the plug is pulled. It leaves out the content of the configuration, retries, multihost, RAID-Z expansion,
        several sync passes in one txg, and the freeing of old blocks, which the <a href={roomUrl(lang, "snapshots")}
        >snapshots room</a> shows. The 3D view draws the same state as the 2D one.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
  <RoomNav lang={lang} roomId="tree" />
</Base>

